DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection. The server must be started with BOTH
  --exec-mode and --exec-logging for the vulnerable endpoint to be reachable.
  A user-supplied URL query parameter flows into system() with no sanitization.

CONFIG_GATE (C2 — struct, two flags):
  mechanism:  CLI flags (--exec-mode AND --exec-logging), stored in struct exec_config exec_cfg
  check:      compound boolean guard inside accept_request()
              `if (exec_cfg.enabled && exec_cfg.logging && strncmp(url, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
  complexity: two conditions, two flags, struct-based config, no file parsing

INPUT_FLOW (I2 — buffered):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  url[] (parsed in accept_request())
  path:       accept_request() dispatches to handle_exec()
              handle_exec() extracts substring after "cmd=" then wraps it in
              snprintf(shell_cmd, 512, "sh -c '%s' 2>/dev/null", cmd)
              the wrapped string is passed to system()
  transforms: one snprintf() wrap — still injectable; > and ; pass through unmodified

SANITIZATION (S1 — none):
  none

SINK:        system() in handle_exec() — cmd wrapped in sh -c shell_cmd buffer via snprintf (httpd.c, injected into accept_request / handle_exec)

CHANGED FILES:
  httpd.c — 4 hunks:
    1. globals:          added `struct exec_config exec_cfg`
    2. send_exec_reply(): new HTTP response helper function
    3. handle_exec():    new function with I2/S1 variant logic
    4. accept_request(): added exec route guard after url[] is read
    5. main():           changed main(void) to main(int argc, char *argv[]) with --port/--exec-mode/--exec-logging parsing

EXPLOIT:
  build:   gcc -o httpd httpd.c -lpthread
  start:   (cd /tmp/poc_013_www && ./httpd --port 18013 --exec-mode --exec-logging)
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18013/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/poc_013_marker'
  verify:  cat /tmp/poc_013_marker
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  gate bypass: omitting either --exec-mode or --exec-logging (or both) causes /exec
               to return 404 (the guard requires both exec_cfg.enabled and exec_cfg.logging).

NOTES:

  - system() output goes to the server's stdout/stderr (inherited fds), not the
    HTTP response body. The response body contains only "Command dispatched.".
    Use a write-to-file payload (id>/tmp/poc_013_marker) to confirm blind execution.
  - I2 wraps cmd in "sh -c '%s' 2>/dev/null". The wrapping does not prevent
    injection via > or ; since they pass through to the shell unchanged.
    Payloads with single quotes would need escaping, but > works directly.
